{"kind":"request","text":"Codex: independently review the repaired remote-link head at exact clean pushed head 512f5c5fc4367797839394b573df1e2944b7d50d, on request/repo-remote-link. This answers your ratified changes-requested c8df0126, under planner's dd883da0 and my promise 29b95948.\n\nYOUR BLOCKER WAS THE ONE THAT MATTERED AND I HAD MISSED IT ENTIRELY. gitRemotes ran git config without --local, so command and global scope contributed remote.origin.url. I reproduced your GIT_CONFIG_COUNT finding before changing anything: the real local origin is emitted first, the injected one second, and gitRemotes keys a map by name, so the injected URL wins. I had built an allowlist for the URL's scheme and never asked whether the URL was the repository's to begin with. Fixed with --local, and followed through three layers by regression: gitRemotes itself, LocalRepo, and the live /v0/worktrees body.\n\nYOU WERE ALSO RIGHT THAT QUERY AND FRAGMENT WERE AN INCONSISTENCY RATHER THAN A GAP. I declined userinfo as credential-bearing and then passed ?access_token=SECRET through. I chose to reject rather than narrow the policy: a repository's own address needs neither component, so refusing costs nothing real, and judging which parameter names are secret would be an enumerated denylist — the shape the scheme rule is careful not to be. Both layers test the serialisation rather than parsed fields, because both parsers escape ? and # inside a host or path, so a bare one can only be the delimiter. A case pins that a percent-encoded ? in a path stays admitted.\n\nSOMETHING YOU SUSPECTED AND I CONFIRMED. The pre-existing org-alias: case never forced an allowlist — it carries no host, so a denylist refuses it too. future+git:// and x-unheard-of:// parse cleanly with a host, so only the scheme rule can refuse them; both are now pinned at four layers, and replacing the allowlist with an enumerated denylist fails a rendered-DOM assertion.\n\nBOUNDS. 64 KiB of config output and 64 remotes, both failing closed rather than truncating, because a truncated set would make which remote gets linked depend on where reading stopped.\n\nDOCUMENTATION. All three pages you named, with line numbers re-derived at this head: docs/reference/gs/serve.md 260-287, docs/concepts/components.md 153-168, and docs/reference/architecture.md 1052-1073 plus the table row at 1124. The architecture edit is the layer-contract change and its candidate artifact is published at that path here. Seven stable paths change and seven artifacts are filed — I under-filed last time and would rather you check the set than take my word.\n\nONE MEASUREMENT I HAVE TO DISCLOSE. My first full suite at this head failed: cmd/gs hit the ten-minute package timeout. That was my own doing — another agent's suite was running and I mutated app.go in this worktree while a background suite read it. I did not dismiss it as flaky. cmd/gs then passed alone at 403.972s, and a clean full run passed with cmd/gs at 461.363s. The figures below are that clean run.\n\nGATES, RUN BY ME AT THIS EXACT HEAD from a run I watched exit. go test ./... -count=1 exit 0, 34 ok plus 1 no-test-files, 35 packages, zero failures and zero cached; npm test --prefix ui 98 tests, 98 pass, 0 fail; gofmt -l . empty; go build ./... and go vet ./... exit 0 with no output; make ui exit 0 leaving git status empty; make ui-check exit 0; git diff --check exit 0. Local and origin identical by commit SHA and tree hash 29ac34a8, checkout clean, main untouched at 02558e8d.\n\nMUTATIONS, BOTH RUN BY ME. Removing --local fails the two outer-scope assertions with the attacker URL where the repository's origin belongs; internal/app/app.go sha256 is byte-identical before and after. Replacing the allowlist with an enumerated denylist fails the unenumerated-scheme assertions at the Go, service and DOM layers.\n\nONE DIVERGENCE RECORDED RATHER THAN FIXED. Go drops a bare trailing # and JavaScript keeps it. Harmless only because the service now never emits either delimiter, so both layers agree on every transported value — and where they differ the browser is stricter. Say if you want them reconciled anyway.","body":{"branch":"request/repo-remote-link","conditions":"Independent review of exact clean pushed head 512f5c5fc4367797839394b573df1e2944b7d50d on request/repo-remote-link, citing the seven live artifacts at that head: d109ea20 internal/app, 4954f1bd internal/service, fca514b9 ui, 892f18df internal/service/uidist, 9bbeaddb docs/reference/architecture.md, 189db63a docs/reference/gs/serve.md, 350406de docs/concepts/components.md. This answers your ratified changes-requested c8df0126; governing request is planner's dd883da0. Record Architecture, Security and Simplification conclusions. SECURITY: judge whether repository-local reading closes the injection you found at every layer, whether the bounds genuinely fail closed, whether rejecting query and fragment is the right call over a narrower credential policy, and whether the allowlist is now pinned against a denylist substitution. ARCHITECTURE: the architecture.md edit is the layer-contract change with its artifact published here; judge whether the page states the contract accurately and whether the two exhaustive-claim corrections are complete. SIMPLIFICATION: judge whether anything here can be cut without weakening a condition. Also check the artifact set covers every changed stable path — I under-filed on the previous head and would rather you verify than trust me. Do not merge; merge is mine and only after your approval is ratified.","head":"512f5c5fc4367797839394b573df1e2944b7d50d","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}